TY  - JOUR
TI  - The Ethical Framework for the Application of Artificial Intelligence in Healthcare from the Perspective of Maqāṣid al-Sharīʿah : An Integrated Approach
AU  - Bin Yunos, Ahmad Safwan
AU  - bin Mohamad Tahir, Zahin
AU  - bin Abdull Rahim, Muhammad Aiman
AU  - bin Hamdan, Mohammad Naqib
PY  - 2026
T2  - Philosophy &amp; Technology
AB  - This study explores the integration of maqāṣid al-sharīʿah , which refers to the higher objectives of Islamic law, within the ethical framework of artificial intelligence (AI) in healthcare. Grounded in the principles of jalb al-maṣāliḥ (promotion of benefit) and darʾ al-mafāsid (prevention of harm), the study examines how classical and contemporary interpretations of maqāṣid can provide a robust moral foundation for emerging technologies, while acknowledging the distinction between jurisprudential methodology and comprehensive ethical philosophy. The traditional five necessities ( al-ḍarūriyyāt al-khams ), namely the preservation of religion ( ḥifẓ al-dīn ), life ( ḥifẓ al-nafs ), intellect ( ḥifẓ al-ʿaql ), lineage and dignity ( ḥifẓ al-nasl wa al-ʿirḍ ), and property ( ḥifẓ al-māl ), are revisited within a selectively extended contemporary model that also recognises modern objectives such as human dignity, justice, privacy, the protection of human rights ( ḥifẓ ḥuqūq al-insān ), and the environment ( ḥifẓ al-bīʾah ). Through a qualitative, library-based methodology, this research analyses classical sources including al-Ghazālī, al-Shāṭibī, and Ibn ʿĀshūr, together with contemporary scholars such as Mohammad Hashim Kamali, Yūsuf al-Qaraḍāwī, and Jasser Auda, to bridge traditional Islamic ethical thought with current AI ethical frameworks. Critically, the study engages with recent philosophical critiques, particularly Taha Abdurrahman’s iʿtimānī (trusteeship) framework as articulated in Ali et al., ( 2025 ), that challenge the elevation of maqāṣid from a juristic hermeneutical tool into an autonomous ethical system. Building on this engagement, the paper clarifies the epistemic status of its maqāṣid -based approach, develops an analysis of moral agency and intention ( niyyah ) in AI-mediated clinical practice, and proposes a multi-level responsibility architecture distinguishing policy, system design and clinical conduct. The findings demonstrate that maqāṣid al-sharīʿah , when supplemented with ontological and epistemological grounding, provides a dynamic and adaptable structure that aligns with universal ethical values such as justice, accountability and transparency and maintaining a distinctive spiritual dimension rooted in divine accountability. In healthcare, these principles translate into safeguarding patient welfare, ensuring informed consent, protecting data privacy and upholding the sanctity of life through responsible AI use. Ultimately, the study proposes an integrated maqāṣid -based ethical model that harmonises Sharīʿah values with global AI ethics, ensuring that technological progress serves both worldly welfare and eternal accountability.
